NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-table"},{"type":"text","value":" adapter wraps the framework-agnostic core with Vue reactivity, so table options can include refs and computed values. This page gets you from install to a rendering Vue table, then shows how to layer on your first feature."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/vue-table","lang":"bash"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"your-first-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your First Table"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The single-file component below is complete. Paste it into a Vue 3 app and you will see a working table."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { h, ref } from 'vue'\nimport { FlexRender, tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/vue-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => h('span', 'Last Name'),\n    cell: (info) => h('i', info.getValue<string>()),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n// 4. Keep your data in a ref so the table reacts to changes\nconst data = ref<Array<Person>>([\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n])\n\n// 5. Create the table instance\nconst table = useTable({\n  key: 'person-table', // registers this table with the devtools\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n</script>\n\n<!-- 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs -->\n<template>\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <tr v-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\" :key=\"headerGroup.id\">\n        <th v-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n          <FlexRender v-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\" :header=\"header\" />\n        </th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr v-for=\"row in table.getRowModel().rows\" :key=\"row.id\">\n        <td v-for=\"cell in row.getAllCells()\" :key=\"cell.id\">\n          <FlexRender :cell=\"cell\" />\n        </td>\n      </tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A few things to note:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core row model is always included. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered directly on the features object when you need them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Passing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" ref directly keeps the table reactive. Reassign "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data.value"},{"type":"text","value":" and the table updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" renders the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":" definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values, render functions using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"h"},{"type":"text","value":", or Vue components."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" option is optional unless you use the "},{"type":"link","href":"../../devtools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table Devtools"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The devtools identify tables by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", and you register a table by calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useTanStackTableDevtools(table)"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-table-devtools"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the full "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-use-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic useTable example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a runnable version with more columns and a footer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a Feature: Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", and wire the header click handler."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { h, ref } from 'vue'\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// columns and data unchanged from above\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  key: 'person-table',\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <tr v-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\" :key=\"headerGroup.id\">\n        <th\n          v-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\"\n          :key=\"header.id\"\n          :style=\"{\n            cursor: header.column.getCanSort() ? 'pointer' : undefined,\n          }\"\n          @click=\"header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()?.($event)\"\n        >\n          <template v-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n            <FlexRender :header=\"header\" />\n            {{\n              { asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' }[header.column.getIsSorted() as string]\n            }}\n          </template>\n        </th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n  </table>\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model factory (if it has one) in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/sorting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to Go Next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table state."}]},{"type":"text","value":" In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, and the Vue adapter wires them into Vue reactivity for you. You usually do not need to manage state yourself. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" for starting values and call feature APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.nextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":". When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/table-state.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". It is the foundational guide for everything else."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature guides."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each feature has its own guide, such as "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-filtering.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filtering"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/pagination.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/row-selection.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/column-visibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Visibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable tables."}]},{"type":"text","value":" When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({\n  features,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/composable-tables.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable Tables Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Browse the runnable "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-use-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vue examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":", from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"your-first-table","text":"Your First Table","level":2},{"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","text":"Add a Feature: Sorting","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to Go Next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. The `@tanstack/vue-table` adapter wraps the framework-agnostic core with Vue reactivity, so table options can include refs and computed values. This page gets you from install to a rendering Vue table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/vue-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe single-file component below is complete. Paste it into a Vue 3 app and you will see a working table.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { h, ref } from 'vue'\nimport { FlexRender, tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/vue-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => h('span', 'Last Name'),\n    cell: (info) => h('i', info.getValue<string>()),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n// 4. Keep your data in a ref so the table reacts to changes\nconst data = ref<Array<Person>>([\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n])\n\n// 5. Create the table instance\nconst table = useTable({\n  key: 'person-table', // registers this table with the devtools\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n</script>\n\n<!-- 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs -->\n<template>\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <tr v-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\" :key=\"headerGroup.id\">\n        <th v-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n          <FlexRender v-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\" :header=\"header\" />\n        </th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr v-for=\"row in table.getRowModel().rows\" :key=\"row.id\">\n        <td v-for=\"cell in row.getAllCells()\" :key=\"cell.id\">\n          <FlexRender :cell=\"cell\" />\n        </td>\n      </tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</template>\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered directly on the features object when you need them.\n- Passing the `data` ref directly keeps the table reactive. Reassign `data.value` and the table updates.\n- `FlexRender` renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values, render functions using `h`, or Vue components.\n- The `key` option is optional unless you use the [TanStack Table Devtools](../../devtools). The devtools identify tables by `key`, and you register a table by calling `useTanStackTableDevtools(table)` from `@tanstack/vue-table-devtools`.\n\nSee the full [Basic useTable example](./examples/basic-use-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and a `sortedRowModel` in `tableFeatures`, and wire the header click handler.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { h, ref } from 'vue'\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// columns and data unchanged from above\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  key: 'person-table',\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <tr v-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\" :key=\"headerGroup.id\">\n        <th\n          v-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\"\n          :key=\"header.id\"\n          :style=\"{\n            cursor: header.column.getCanSort() ? 'pointer' : undefined,\n          }\"\n          @click=\"header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()?.($event)\"\n        >\n          <template v-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n            <FlexRender :header=\"header\" />\n            {{\n              { asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' }[header.column.getIsSorted() as string]\n            }}\n          </template>\n        </th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n  </table>\n</template>\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model factory (if it has one) in `tableFeatures`, then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, and the Vue adapter wires them into Vue reactivity for you. You usually do not need to manage state yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({\n  features,\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Vue examples](./examples/basic-use-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Quick Start\n---\n\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. The `@tanstack/vue-table` adapter wraps the framework-agnostic core with Vue reactivity, so table options can include refs and computed values. This page gets you from install to a rendering Vue table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/vue-table\n```\n\n## Your First Table\n\nThe single-file component below is complete. Paste it into a Vue 3 app and you will see a working table.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { h, ref } from 'vue'\nimport { FlexRender, tableFeatures, useTable } from '@tanstack/vue-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => h('span', 'Last Name'),\n    cell: (info) => h('i', info.getValue<string>()),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n// 4. Keep your data in a ref so the table reacts to changes\nconst data = ref<Array<Person>>([\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n])\n\n// 5. Create the table instance\nconst table = useTable({\n  key: 'person-table', // registers this table with the devtools\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n</script>\n\n<!-- 6. Render markup from the table instance APIs -->\n<template>\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <tr v-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\" :key=\"headerGroup.id\">\n        <th v-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n          <FlexRender v-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\" :header=\"header\" />\n        </th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr v-for=\"row in table.getRowModel().rows\" :key=\"row.id\">\n        <td v-for=\"cell in row.getAllCells()\" :key=\"cell.id\">\n          <FlexRender :cell=\"cell\" />\n        </td>\n      </tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</template>\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered directly on the features object when you need them.\n- Passing the `data` ref directly keeps the table reactive. Reassign `data.value` and the table updates.\n- `FlexRender` renders the `header`, `cell`, and `footer` definitions from your columns, whether they are plain values, render functions using `h`, or Vue components.\n- The `key` option is optional unless you use the [TanStack Table Devtools](../../devtools). The devtools identify tables by `key`, and you register a table by calling `useTanStackTableDevtools(table)` from `@tanstack/vue-table-devtools`.\n\nSee the full [Basic useTable example](./examples/basic-use-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and a `sortedRowModel` in `tableFeatures`, and wire the header click handler.\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { h, ref } from 'vue'\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n  useTable,\n} from '@tanstack/vue-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// columns and data unchanged from above\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  key: 'person-table',\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <tr v-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\" :key=\"headerGroup.id\">\n        <th\n          v-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\"\n          :key=\"header.id\"\n          :style=\"{\n            cursor: header.column.getCanSort() ? 'pointer' : undefined,\n          }\"\n          @click=\"header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()?.($event)\"\n        >\n          <template v-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n            <FlexRender :header=\"header\" />\n            {{\n              { asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' }[header.column.getIsSorted() as string]\n            }}\n          </template>\n        </th>\n      </tr>\n    </thead>\n    <!-- tbody unchanged from above -->\n  </table>\n</template>\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature and its row model factory (if it has one) in `tableFeatures`, then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows. See the [Sorting Guide](./guide/sorting.md) and the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, and the Vue adapter wires them into Vue reactivity for you. You usually do not need to manage state yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When your app should own a state slice, or you want fine-grained subscriptions, read the [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md). It is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature guides.** Each feature has its own guide, such as [Column Filtering](./guide/column-filtering.md), [Pagination](./guide/pagination.md), [Row Selection](./guide/row-selection.md), and [Column Visibility](./guide/column-visibility.md).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features, row models, and component conventions, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { useAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({\n  features,\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Composable Tables Guide](./guide/composable-tables.md) for the full pattern, including pre-bound cell and header components.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Vue examples](./examples/basic-use-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n"}
